// The library's answer for the same gradient: refused by name, and the other half of the anchor.
//
// `docs/handover/reports/2026-11-03-what-deleting-the-engine-pullback-takes.cn.md` (D1) asks that
// every operator the engine can differentiate gain a cotangent rule in
// `stdlib/rulesets/transpose.cml`. `^` on two floats is the first member of that difference set:
// its forward rule `jvp_pow` is written (`stdlib/rulesets/jvp.cml`, and the forward half runs here
// -- the reading says `rules=jvp_pow`), the reverse rule `pow_t` is not, so `vjp` -- which is
// `transpose(split(jvp(f)))` -- stops where the transposition needs it:
//
//     REINTERP jvp function=p graph=250 … rules=jvp_pow
//     REINTERP split function=jvp_of_p forward=266 backward=267 type=(float) => (float, (float) => float pure) pure residuals=3
//     TRANSPOSE function=rulesets.jvp::pow_tangent<float> skip=rule uri=math:pow_d reason=unbound
//     TRANSPOSE function=split_of_jvp_of_p_backward skip=call-no-transpose node=6 callee=rulesets.jvp::pow_tangent<float>
//
//     transforms: transpose has no transpose for this function: this call's callee has no transpose
//     of its own, so the call cannot be turned around
//
// The refused key is `math:pow_d` -- the scalar spelling `^` puts in the graph -- and `unbound` is
// the rule table's own word for "this family has no rule for that operator". The transposition
// stops at `pow_tangent`, the library function the forward rule answers with, which is the other
// half of the same fact: a cotangent rule is written for the primal, not for its tangent function.
//
// The engine's answer for the same function is the neighbouring
// `pow_gradient_through_the_engine.cml`. The two readings cannot share one program: this refusal
// happens where the graph is built, so the program never runs and the engine's number would never
// be printed. The day a `pow_t` is written this case fails and names the sentence that replaced
// this one.
//
// The forward half of `^` -- what `jvp_pow` answers, the constant exponent's zero tangent,
// `log(-3.0)` kept out of the arithmetic -- is already pinned by `jvp_of_a_power.cml`, and nothing
// here repeats it.
//
//     camel test/cases/compile/transforms/pow_gradient_refused_by_the_library.cml
import { vjp } from vjp

/// The library's spelling: the differentiated value is an ordinary parameter (design principles
/// 9.9), so `vjp` transforms it and the pullback answers its cotangent.
func p(x: float): float {
    return x ^ 2.0
}

func main(): int sync {
    let (value, pullback) = vjp(p)(3.0)
    let cotangents = pullback(1.0)
    `pow library value={} dL/dx={}`->format<value, cotangents.0>->println
    return 0
}